<Page
    title="Sudoku - Classic Puzzle Game – Privacy Policy"
    description="How I handle your data when using Sudoku - Classic Puzzle Game."
    headline="Sudoku - Classic Puzzle Game"
>
    <h2 id="privacy-policy">Privacy Policy</h2>
    <p>Version 2026-08-11. Effective 11 August 2026.</p>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
        I run Sudoku personally, not as a company, so the person responsible for
        everything here is me, Edvin Lindén. Write to <a
            href="mailto:hello@edvinlinden.se">hello@edvinlinden.se</a
        >.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you email me, your message and address stay in my email archive.
        Nothing goes on a mailing list, nothing is shared, and I'll delete a
        message on request.
    </p>

    <h2>What Sudoku collects</h2>
    <p>
        Sudoku collects usage analytics. With analytics switched on, this is
        what is collected:
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>An identifier for your installation of Sudoku.</strong> It cannot
        be traced back to you, and it cannot be matched against any other app, including
        my own.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>When it happened.</strong> An identifier for the current session,
        and the date and time.
    </p>
    <p><strong>What happened.</strong> Every event Sudoku sends:</p>
    <ul style="list-style:disc; margin-left:32px;">
        <li>your first session after installing the app</li>
        <li>a session starting</li>
        <li>
            finishing the first-launch introduction, and which of its three
            routes you took
        </li>
        <li>starting a new puzzle, with the difficulty</li>
        <li>resuming a puzzle you had already started</li>
        <li>completing a puzzle, with the difficulty and how long it took</li>
        <li>
            giving up on a puzzle by starting another one, with the difficulty,
            how long you played and how far through it you got
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        <strong>How many puzzles you have solved.</strong> The number, attached to
        every one of the events above.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>What the app is running on.</strong> The device model, operating system,
        screen size, language, region and time zone, whether the app is in light or
        dark mode, its version, and whether it came from the App Store or from a test
        build.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>How much you use the app.</strong> The date you first used it, how
        many sessions you have had, how long they last, and how many days you have
        opened it.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Technical details of the analytics software</strong>, such as
        its version.
    </p>

    <h2>What Sudoku keeps on your device</h2>
    <p>
        Analytics keeps a small record of when you have opened the app, which is
        what the usage counts above are worked out from. Events waiting to be
        sent are held on the device too, until they're delivered. Turning
        analytics off deletes both. If you never turn analytics on, it is never
        written.
    </p>
    <p>
        Sudoku also stores your analytics decision: whether it is on or off,
        when and how you decided, the version of this policy in force at the
        time, and the country of your App Store account. The country decides
        whether you have to be asked for consent, and is never sent anywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>What Sudoku syncs through your iCloud</h2>
    <p>
        Your puzzles in progress, your statistics and your settings are stored
        on your device. If iCloud sync is on — it is on by default, and there is
        a switch for it under More, then Settings — they are also stored in your
        own iCloud account, so you can start a puzzle on one device and finish
        it on another.
    </p>
    <p>
        That sync goes between your devices and Apple's servers. It does not go
        to me, and I have no access to it. Apple's handling of it is covered by
        Apple's privacy policy, not this one.
    </p>
    <p>
        Nothing about your puzzles or statistics is sent to me, with one
        exception, which is stated above and worth repeating plainly: the
        analytics events include the difficulty of a puzzle you start, finish or
        give up on, how long a puzzle took, how many of its squares you had
        filled in when you gave up, and how many puzzles you have solved in
        total. The boards themselves, your notes and your entries are never
        sent.
    </p>

    <h2>What Sudoku doesn't collect</h2>
    <p>
        No name, email or account, because Sudoku has no accounts. No
        advertising identifier, no ad networks. No tracking across other
        companies' apps or websites. No GPS or location services data. Nothing
        about your accessibility settings. I do not sell data or share it with
        advertisers or brokers.
    </p>
    <p>
        Nothing here is used to make automated decisions about you, and nothing
        builds a profile of you. The data answers questions about groups of
        players, not about individuals.
    </p>

    <h2>Why Sudoku collects this data</h2>
    <p>Three reasons, and no others:</p>
    <ol style="list-style:decimal; margin-left:32px;">
        <li>
            Checking a release works, by seeing whether the app is still used
            normally afterwards.
        </li>
        <li>
            Seeing which features people find and use, and whether the
            difficulty levels are graded sensibly, so I know what to improve.
        </li>
        <li>
            Counting users by country, to decide which languages to translate
            into.
        </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        In the European Economic Area the legal basis is your consent, Article
        6(1)(a) GDPR. Sudoku asks first, sends nothing until you say yes, and
        you can withdraw any time. Everywhere else it is my legitimate
        interests, Article 6(1)(f), those interests being the three purposes
        above, and analytics is on by default with a switch to turn it off.
    </p>

    <h2>Opting out</h2>
    <p>
        The switch is under More and then Privacy, in the same place whether you
        were asked for consent or not. Turning it off ends collection
        immediately, deletes anything that had not yet been sent, and deletes
        what analytics had stored on your device. Sudoku works the same with
        analytics off. Withdrawing consent does not make what was collected
        beforehand unlawful, it only stops collection from that point.
    </p>
    <p>
        Turning it back on records a fresh decision against the current policy
        version.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the data goes and how long it stays</h2>
    <p>
        Data goes to TelemetryDeck GmbH in Augsburg, Germany, which provides the
        analytics service. Their privacy policy is at <a
            href="https://telemetrydeck.com/privacy"
            title="TelemetryDeck - Privacy Policy"
            target="_blank">https://telemetrydeck.com/privacy</a
        >.
    </p>
    <p>
        TelemetryDeck stores it in the EU and does not transfer it outside the
        EU or the EEA. TelemetryDeck keeps it available for 24 months, and after
        that moves it to cold storage rather than deleting it.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
        You have the right to access your data, correct it, delete it, restrict
        its use, object to its use, receive a copy, and withdraw your consent.
        Email me.
    </p>
    <p>
        The analytics data holds nothing that identifies you, so I can't find
        your records in it. Article 11 GDPR doesn't require me to start
        collecting more about you to make that possible, and I won't. What I can
        do is stop collecting from you, which is what the switch above does.
    </p>
    <p>
        You have the right to lodge a complaint with the Swedish data protection
        authority, Integritetsskyddsmyndigheten (IMY), or with the supervisory
        authority where you live.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
        This policy may be updated. Changes are published on this page with a
        new effective date.
    </p>
</Page>
